  "version": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Work as One Whole: Time, Rate, and Remaining Work",
    "description": "Turn job time into work rate and track completed or remaining work.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with a job represented by the number 1, then make completion time and one-day work feel like reciprocals. Build through work done over several days, remaining work, combining rates, partial-job statements, and more-than-one-unit output. Every question asks the learner to identify which of the three quantities—rate, time, work—is missing.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 17, Time and Work. Approved scope: Teach the chapter's core model: take the whole job as 1. If A finishes in n days, A's one-day work is 1/n; if a worker completes only a stated fraction in some days, first recover the whole-job time or daily rate. Cover work completed in several days, remaining work, two and three workers combining, and jobs larger than one unit such as producing two identical items. Emphasize rate × time = work and work ÷ rate = time."
  },
  "scriptId": "tw-01-work-as-one-whole-rate-basics",
